# ApexYard

> ApexYard gives your agents a team's process, then checks every pull request against it. The framework is open source (MIT).

## Your agents build. The gate decides what ships.

ApexYard gives your agents a team's process, then checks every pull request against it.

Agent agnostic. Works with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode, Pi and omp.

## One process, three places

Keel is your team's rules and context. Build, the Gate and Readiness all work from it. A miss comes back as a new rule.

1. **Keel**: Every API endpoint checks who is calling. A rule your team adds to its handbook, marked blocking.
2. **Build**: Before an agent writes code, it loads your rules. It writes each new endpoint with an auth check.
3. **Gate**: Rex, the review agent, checks each pull request against the rule. A blocking rule that fails stops the merge.
4. **Readiness**: The readiness audit fails when a user-facing app has no authentication. On me2resh/ddd-sam-example, the top action was: add authentication to all patient appointment APIs.
5. **A miss becomes a rule**: When a person finds a miss in review, /codify-rule turns the comment into a draft rule. You approve it before it is saved.

- About Build: <https://apexyard.ai/build>
- About Gate: <https://apexyard.ai/gate>
- About Readiness: <https://apexyard.ai/readiness>

Underneath: Keel, one evidence store and one dashboard.

20 roles, 69 skills, 23 agents and 66 hooks.

From ApexYard building itself, counted on 14 November 2026 in the public repository me2resh/apexyard:

- 699 merged pull requests with an approving review: <https://github.com/me2resh/apexyard/pulls?q=is%3Apr+is%3Amerged+review%3Aapproved>
- 289 decision records, one file each: <https://github.com/me2resh/apexyard/tree/dev/docs/agdr>
- 334 merged pull requests with a comment that says CHANGES REQUESTED: <https://github.com/me2resh/apexyard/pulls?q=is%3Apr+is%3Amerged+%22CHANGES+REQUESTED%22+in%3Acomments>

Our servers never fetch your code. The GitHub App sees pull request titles, descriptions and changed file names, not the files. See what the GitHub App can see: <https://apexyard.ai/gate#app-access>.

## Install Build

Open source. MIT. Always free.

The hosted parts are paid. Every rule, hook and review agent is a plain file you can read, change or remove.

Install the command line tool, then create your private ops repository. The command asks for the name and the account:

```sh
brew install me2resh/tap/apexyard && apexyard init
```

For scripts, use the flag form in the [docs](/docs).

```sh
npx create-apexyard --org <your-org> --name <new-private-repo> --private
```

## Book a demo

Bring one of your repositories, and see what the Gate would have held. Book from <https://apexyard.ai/#book-a-demo>.

Build is free and open source. Pay when a team needs the hosted Gate, shared Keel and evidence. See plans: <https://apexyard.ai/pricing>.

